  "summary_text": "Kyle,\n\nYesterday was a scheduling miss, not a performance miss. Port Washington ran 4 groomers and produced $480 per groomer\u20142% below the 30-day baseline of $503, which is noise. Hicksville ran 1 groomer at $440, which is 6% above baseline. Both locations delivered solid per-groomer productivity despite the brutal top-line delta (Port down 47%, Hicksville down 55% YoY). The real story: Port had zero bather hours yesterday when the trailing average is 4.3h/day, and Hicksville ran skeleton crew at 1 groomer vs. the 2.0/day norm. This was a capacity scheduling decision, not operational decay.\n\nMonth-to-date, Port is exactly on plan. Actual MTD revenue of $60,560 sits 3.6% below DOW-adjusted expectation of $62,794\u2014calendar timing accounts for the gap, not team performance. Year-to-date Port is up 48% ($562k vs. $381k last year), the strongest leading indicator we have. Hicksville is outperforming: actual MTD $21,504 beats DOW-adjusted expectation by 4.4%, and per-groomer productivity is up 4.4% month-over-month ($423 vs. $405). Be proud of that.\n\nOne concern: Hicksville's 12+ week visit cadence is drifting upward (10.2% \u2192 12.6%), signaling stealth churn. Port's is improving (15.5% \u2192 13.3%), which is the right direction. You have 84 dormant or lapsed customers at Hicksville representing $61k in annual value\u2014that's your reactivation pool. Port's lapse pool is larger ($317k) but the trend is working for you there.\n\n**Capacity & coverage: Today Port has 6 groomer-days with 7.0h bathers and Hicksville has 2 groomer-days with 0h bathers; next 7 days Port schedules 34 groomer-days (43.0h bathers) and Hicksville schedules 13 groomer-days (0h bathers).**"
